  "body": "Adds `submissions/c-iouring-tigokraft/`: the Conduit API in C on io_uring, with a hand-written pipelined Postgres wire client. **Front: direct.**\n\n## What it is\n- Event loops on io_uring (multishot accept, multishot readiness poll + `recv()`). Idle keep-alive connections own no buffer, about 200 bytes each.\n- A Postgres v3 protocol client with SCRAM-SHA-256 and every statement prepared once per connection. Each request is one pipelined batch with one Sync. The response waits for that batch's ReadyForQuery, so writes are committed first.\n- SQL rewrites return the same results as SPEC.md's reference SQL with less work:\n  - list pages pick their ids before the per-article subqueries run;\n  - tag names are looked up by primary key;\n  - look-up-then-act pairs are combined into one batch.\n- Session-level settings on its own connections only: `plan_cache_mode=force_generic_plan`, `jit=off`, `max_parallel_workers_per_gather=0`.\n- bcrypt cost 10 for every hash and every login, computed up to 4 at a time per thread for throughput. It is checked against libxcrypt by `bin/conduit --bcrypt-selftest`.\n- Design notes, every optimization with its measured effect, and the dead ends are in the submission's README.\n\n## Rules checklist\n- Rules 2 and 3: Postgres at `$DATABASE_URL` only. At most 95 connections (default 4 per CPU). No schema, server or role changes; only session-level settings at connection startup.\n- Rule 4: passes `test/run.sh`, 197/197.\n- Rule 5: no caching across requests. Only connections, prepared statements and the HMAC key schedule are kept.\n- Rule 6: each batch commits at its Sync before the response is sent. No `synchronous_commit` changes, no unlogged tables.\n- Rule 7: bcrypt cost 10 on register and password change. Every login is verified against the stored hash. Every token's HS256 signature and `exp` are verified on every request.\n- Rule 8: no hard-coded responses and no benchmark detection.\n- Rule 11: `install.sh` installs `build-essential liburing-dev libcrypt-dev` from the Ubuntu 24.04 archive. `build.sh` is one `gcc` call. No downloads and no binaries committed.\n- Rule 12: no kernel tuning and no CPU pinning; only in-process settings (thread counts, pool sizes).\n- Only files under `submissions/c-iouring-tigokraft/` are changed.\n\n## How it was checked\n- `test/run.sh` 197/197 in each of these setups:\n  - an Ubuntu 24.04 VM set up with `box/setup-box.sh`, on the dev and the full seed;\n  - the same VM with the batch queue forced on every request;\n  - a clean `ubuntu:24.04` container against the `postgres:18` image, mirroring this repo's CI: `install.sh` as root, then the tests as a non-root user.\n- A differential test against `examples/go` on the full seed: 1,740+ read requests covering all list filters, deep offsets, the heaviest feeds and articles, both anonymous and logged in. JSON compared with sorted keys: 0 differences.\n- Load tests with `bench/cmd/loadgen` on an i5-9500 VM. Postgres and the server had 4 cores, so the numbers are not comparable with the official box:\n  - a 5-minute hold at 10,000 users with think time x0.1: 12.8k req/s, p95 458 ms, p99 485 ms, 0 errors;\n  - 80,000 open connections: p99 10.6 ms, 0 errors.\n\n\ud83e\udd16 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
